  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 128 AM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nShower and storm chances through Wednesday...All eyes on SD early\nthis morning as this is where the genesis area for storms that \neventually affect WI this morning. Thus far, models are too far \neast with main development which is instead focusing farther west \nin strongest 850mb warm air advection. Maybe that changes given \nthe swath of instability that is present from the central Plains \ninto the Ohio Valley. Right on cue starting to see new development\nover far southeast SD. Utilizing the recent runs of the HRRR that\nare the better depiction from the CAMs suggests that only the far\nsouth would get clipped with more notable showers and storms this\nmorning (maximum coverage 9a to noon) with highest pops 30-50%. \nThere are radar echoes that are over MN with mid-level moisture \nand elevated instability, but thus far just a few sprinkles are \nmaking it to the surface. Still appears that even if our area sees\nstorms, main severe risk will stay south. Later today, pop- up \nshowers and isolated storms (20-30%) may form over the far \nnortheast, but better chances may end up in Upper Michigan. \n\nAnother potential for showers and storms occurs on Wednesday, but\nensembles have trended toward less of a signal. Favored area would\nbe northern WI. Similar to the last few episodes, the primary area\nof instability remains locked up along main frontal boundary and\ncloser to nose of LLJ that never makes it this far north. Result\nare lower chancy pops (25-40%), but also hints that main activity\ncould split our area. Perhaps enough overlap of instability up to\n1000J/kg and shear over 40 kts during peak daytime heating to\nconjure up an isolated severe storm. Based on HREF severe probs \nand model QPF, at this point think this risk is on the lower side.\n\nEven smaller chances for showers/storms on Thursday (10-20%), \nbefore high pressure builds across late this week leading to dry \nweather Thursday night into Friday. This high eventually shifts \neast, allowing the chance for showers and storms to return Friday \nnight through Saturday night. Early look points to minimal \ninstability and thus not much risk for severe. Machine learning \noutput is in agreement with that as well. High pressure builds \nback in for Sunday/Monday bringing mainly dry weather. \n\nTemperatures/Fog...Fog along Lake Michigan will be dense this\nmorning. Moisture and light east flow with nighttime cooling will\nis also resulting in patchy dense fog farther inland toward Fox\nValley and over northeast WI. Similar to last night, may need SPS\nand/or Dense Fog Advisory. Unlike last night, do have mid clouds\napproaching which could cut down on the potential. Will monitor\ntrends. Another round of fog in store tonight. MOS guidance likes\nthe potential for locally dense fog yet again. Getting to be that\ntime of year. Temperatures will be seasonably warm (average highs\nnow are already starting to slip into the upper 70s central and \nnorth- central) with highs in the lower 80s through Thursday. \nSlight cooling into the mid to upper 70s still pegged for Friday \ninto the weekend. Not a big cool down by any stretch. Does still \nappear that Thu night could be chilly up north with lows in the \n40s, and with that there will probably be more fog.",
